🚀 PHASE 5: Deploy Your First Application on EKS

Now we move to the most exciting part:

Dockerized App
      ↓
Kubernetes Deployment
      ↓
Service
      ↓
Access Application

🎯 Goal

Deploy a real containerized app on EKS.

You’ll learn:

Pods
Deployments
Services
LoadBalancer
kubectl workflow

🧠 What We’ll Deploy

For now:

Simple NGINX application

Later:

Real microservices
Backend APIs
Databases
CI/CD

🔍 STEP 1: Verify Cluster Connectivity

Run:

kubectl get nodes

Expected:

STATUS = Ready

🔍 STEP 2: Create Namespace

Namespaces logically separate applications.

Run:

kubectl create namespace ecommerce

Verify:

kubectl get namespaces

📁 STEP 3: Create Kubernetes Folder Structure

Inside project root:

mkdir -p k8s/nginx
cd k8s/nginx

✍️ STEP 4: Create Deployment YAML

Create file:

touch deployment.yaml

apiVersion: apps/v1
kind: Deployment

metadata:
  name: nginx-deployment
  namespace: ecommerce

spec:
  replicas: 2

  selector:
    matchLabels:
      app: nginx

  template:
    metadata:
      labels:
        app: nginx

    spec:
      containers:
      - name: nginx
        image: nginx:latest

        ports:
        - containerPort: 80

▶️ STEP 5: Deploy Application

Run:

kubectl apply -f deployment.yaml
🔍 STEP 6: Verify Deployment

Run:

kubectl get deployments -n ecommerce

Expected:

READY   UP-TO-DATE   AVAILABLE
2/2

STEP 7: Verify Pods

Run:

kubectl get pods -n ecommerce

Expected:

2 Running pods

🧠 What Happened Internally?

Kubernetes:

Deployment
   ↓
ReplicaSet
   ↓
Pods
   ↓
Containers

🔥 STEP 8: Expose Application

Now create Service.

Create file:

touch service.yaml

apiVersion: v1
kind: Service

metadata:
  name: nginx-service
  namespace: ecommerce

spec:
  selector:
    app: nginx

  ports:
    - protocol: TCP
      port: 80
      targetPort: 80

  type: LoadBalancer

🧠 Why Service?

Pods are temporary.

Service gives:

Stable networking
Load balancing
Single access point

🧠 Why LoadBalancer?

Because on AWS:

Service Type LoadBalancer
        ↓
Creates AWS ELB automatically

This is huge interview knowledge.

▶️ STEP 9: Apply Service

Run:

kubectl apply -f service.yaml
🔍 STEP 10: Verify Service

Run:

kubectl get svc -n ecommerce

Initially:

EXTERNAL-IP = pending

Wait 2–5 mins.

Then:

EXTERNAL-IP = <AWS LoadBalancer DNS>

🌐 STEP 11: Access Application

Copy EXTERNAL-IP.

Open in browser:

http://<external-ip>

You should see:

Welcome to nginx!
